                "To solve it, replace Θ(n) by cn, where c &gt; 0 is a constant (slide 1.41). We assume n is a power of 2, so that halving always gives whole numbers ‡.",
                "A <b>recursion tree</b> draws each recursive call as a node labelled with the work done in that call, not counting its children.",
                "Build it (slides 1.42–1.45):<ul><li>T(n) becomes a root cn with two children T(n/2).</li><li>Each T(n/2) becomes cn/2 with two children T(n/4).</li><li>Keep going until the size is 1, where each node costs Θ(1).</li></ul>",
