                "<b>Worst case</b>, W(n) (slide 1.68): the basic operation runs at most n times, when x is the last item or is not in the array. So <b>W(n) = n</b>. It tells us the absolute maximum time.",
                "<b>Average case</b>, A(n): the average (expected value) of the number of times the basic operation is done for an input of size n.",
                "Average when x is in S (slide 1.69): each slot k has probability 1/n, and finding x in slot k takes k comparisons.",
                "The sum, one step per line:<ul><li>A(n) = Σ<sub>k=1</sub><sup>n</sup> k × (1/n)</li><li>= (1/n) × n(n + 1)/2</li><li>= (n + 1)/2.</li></ul>For n = 6: 3.5 comparisons.",
                "If x might be absent (slide 1.69 only names the probabilities): let p be the probability that x is in S, and 1 − p that it is not. An absent x costs n comparisons.",
                "Then ‡:<ul><li>A(n) = p(n + 1)/2 + (1 − p)n</li><li>= pn/2 + p/2 + n − pn</li><li>= n − pn/2 + p/2</li><li>= n(1 − p/2) + p/2.</li></ul>Check: p = 1 gives (n + 1)/2, and p = 0 gives n. For n = 6, p = 1/2: 6 × 0.75 + 0.25 = 4.75.",
