                "<b>Rule #2</b> (slide 2.49): O(f(n)) · O(g(n)) = O(f(n) · g(n)). It applies when an algorithm of cost O(f(n)) calls another of cost O(g(n)). Nested loops are the common case.",
                "Why, with constants, for non-negative functions: a(n)·b(n) ≤ c₁f(n) · c₂g(n) = (c₁c₂)·f(n)g(n) for n ≥ max(n₁, n₂). ‡",
