                "<b>Recipe</b>:<ul><li>1. Root = the outside work f(n).</li><li>2. Add one child per recursive call, with its own size and outside work.</li><li>3. Sum each row.</li><li>4. Find how the rows shrink or grow (the ratio) and how many there are.</li><li>5. Add up the rows with a series.</li><li>6. If you need a proof, confirm the guess by substitution. ‡</li></ul>",
                "Each node holds only the cost at that level of recursion, not the cost of its subtree.",
                "Slide 3.22: T(n) = T(n/4) + T(n/2) + n<sup>2</sup>. The root is n<sup>2</sup>, with children (n/4)<sup>2</sup> and (n/2)<sup>2</sup>.",
                "Row 1: (n/4)<sup>2</sup> + (n/2)<sup>2</sup> = n<sup>2</sup>/16 + 4n<sup>2</sup>/16 = (5/16)n<sup>2</sup>.",
                "Row 2: (n/16)<sup>2</sup> + (n/8)<sup>2</sup> + (n/8)<sup>2</sup> + (n/4)<sup>2</sup> = n<sup>2</sup>(1 + 4 + 4 + 16)/256 = (25/256)n<sup>2</sup> = (5/16)<sup>2</sup>n<sup>2</sup>.",
                "Why it keeps the ratio 5/16: a node of cost m<sup>2</sup> has children (m/4)<sup>2</sup> + (m/2)<sup>2</sup> = (5/16)m<sup>2</sup>. So every row is 5/16 of the row above.",
                "The branches end in leaves of cost Θ(1), which the slide draws at the bottom of the left branch. Leaves are small calls, so they are not counted as (size)<sup>2</sup>.",
                "The rows of cost (size)<sup>2</sup> form a geometric series with ratio 5/16 &lt; 1, so the first term wins:<ul><li>Rows = n<sup>2</sup> + (5/16)n<sup>2</sup> + (5/16)<sup>2</sup>n<sup>2</sup> + …</li><li>= n<sup>2</sup>(1 + 5/16 + (5/16)<sup>2</sup> + (5/16)<sup>3</sup> + …)</li><li>≤ n<sup>2</sup> · 1/(1 − 5/16)</li><li>= (16/11)n<sup>2</sup></li></ul>The Θ(1) leaves add only a lower-order term (about n<sup>0.69</sup> leaves ‡), so T(n) = <b>Θ(n<sup>2</sup>)</b>.",
