              "h": "Before you start: loop invariants",
              "callout": "Background the slides assume. It is not on the slides, but you need it to follow them.",
              "pts": [
                "A <b>loop invariant</b> is a statement about the variables that is true every time the loop is about to run an iteration.",
                "It is proved in three parts:<ul><li><b>Initialization</b>: it is true before the first iteration.</li><li><b>Maintenance</b>: if it is true before an iteration, it is still true before the next one.</li><li><b>Termination</b>: when the loop stops, the invariant gives the result we wanted.</li></ul>",
                "This is the same idea as induction (Lecture 2): initialization is the base case and maintenance is the inductive step.",
                "Tiny example, find the largest value of A[1..n]: <code>m ← A[1]; for j ← 2 to n: if A[j] &gt; m then m ← A[j]</code>. Invariant: before each iteration, m is the largest of A[1..j−1].",
                "Take A = 3 8 5. Initialization: j = 2, A[1..1] = 3, and m = 3. True.",
                "Maintenance, j = 2: A[2] = 8 is larger than m = 3, so m ← 8, the largest of A[1..2].",
                "Maintenance, j = 3: A[3] = 5 is not larger, so m stays 8, still the largest of A[1..3].",
                "Termination: the loop stops at j = n + 1 = 4. The invariant says m is the largest of A[1..3]: exactly what we wanted.",
                "PARTITION has its own invariant, drawn on Slide 5.4. The section \"PARTITION and its loop invariant\" proves it the same way."
              ],
              "lead": "How can we be sure a loop does what we claim?"
