                "Each level can at most double. The root is 1 node, the level below has at most 2 nodes, the next at most 4, and level d at most 2<sup>d</sup>. So a tree of height h has at most <b>2<sup>h</sup> leaves</b>.",
                "<b>Proof by induction</b> shows a statement for every h in two moves: prove it for the smallest h (base case), then prove that if it holds for h − 1 it also holds for h (induction step). Radix sort's correctness (slides 6.33–6.35) uses the same idea on digit positions.",
                "The induction for leaves:<ul><li>base case h = 0: the tree is one node, which is a leaf, and 1 = 2<sup>0</sup></li><li>step: a tree of height h has a root and at most two subtrees, each of height ≤ h − 1</li><li>by the induction hypothesis each subtree has at most 2<sup>h−1</sup> leaves</li><li>total: at most 2 × 2<sup>h−1</sup> = 2<sup>h</sup> leaves</li></ul>",
