                "If k is large it becomes slow: with k = n<sup>2</sup> the time is Θ(n<sup>2</sup>), and 32-bit keys would need k ≈ 4 billion counters even for 5 numbers. This is why radix sort splits keys into digits. ‡",
                "The fallacy of slide 6.29: \"k = O(n) gives Θ(n), but sorting takes Ω(n lg n)!\" Where is the mistake?",
