                "Case 1 (slide 6.34): the two numbers <b>differ in digit t</b>. The pass orders them by digit t, and digit t outranks all lower digits, so they end up correctly sorted. Example with t = 3: 720 and 329 have hundreds digits 7 and 3, so 329 moves above 720.",
                "Case 2 (slide 6.35): the two numbers are <b>equal in digit t</b>. The sort is stable, so they stay in the same order as in the input. By the assumption that input order was already correct on the low-order t − 1 digits, so the order is correct.",
