                "Assume counting sort is the auxiliary stable sort, and we sort n computer words of b bits each (slide 6.36).",
                "Each word can be viewed as having <b>b/r</b> digits in base 2<sup>r</sup>: cut the b bits into pieces of r bits. Each piece is one digit, and each digit needs one pass.",
                "Example on the slide: a 32-bit word. With r = 8 it is 4 digits (8 8 8 8), so <b>4 passes</b> of counting sort on base-2<sup>8</sup> digits. With r = 16 it is 2 digits, so <b>2 passes</b> on base-2<sup>16</sup> digits.",
                "The question of slide 6.36: how many passes should we make?",
                "Recall (slide 6.37): counting sort takes Θ(n + k) time to sort n numbers in the range from 0 to k − 1. An r-bit digit has values 0 to 2<sup>r</sup> − 1, so k = <b>2<sup>r</sup></b> and each pass costs Θ(n + 2<sup>r</sup>).",
                "Two conventions: slide 6.11 uses keys 1..k and slide 6.37 uses 0..k − 1. Same cost, just shifted by one (add 1 to every digit, or index C from 0). Some books write k = 2<sup>r</sup> − 1: that is the largest digit value, while on these slides k counts the values, so k = 2<sup>r</sup>. ‡",
                "There are b/r passes, so <b>T(n, b) = Θ((b/r)(n + 2<sup>r</sup>))</b>.",
