                "An <b>indicator variable</b> is 1 if an event happens and 0 if not. Example: I = 1 if the die shows 6, otherwise 0.",
                "E[I] = 1·P(event) + 0·P(no event) = P(event). For the die, E[I] = 1/6. An indicator's expected value is the probability of its event.",
                "<b>Linearity of expectation</b>: E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y] and E[aX] = a·E[X] for a constant a. This always holds, even when X and Y depend on each other. Two dice: 3.5 + 3.5 = 7.",
                "So E can move through a whole sum: E[Σ X<sub>k</sub>] = Σ E[X<sub>k</sub>].",
                "<b>Independent</b> variables are ones where knowing one tells you nothing about the other. For independent variables, E[XY] = E[X]·E[Y] is guaranteed. Without independence it can fail.",
                "Example: X, Y are indicators of two separate fair coins showing heads. E[XY] = P(both heads) = 1/4 = (1/2)(1/2).",
                "The rule really needs independence: X·X = X, so E[X·X] = 1/2, not (1/2)(1/2) = 1/4. X and X are not independent of each other.",
