                "<b>Extra Problem 3: Successive $k$-Way Merge Sort (Lab 3 PDF p. 6, Midterm 2017 Q3, Final 2019 Credit Q1 & Final Summer Q27)</b>:<ul><li><b>Problem</b>: Given $k$ sorted arrays, each of size $n$ (total $kn$ elements), suppose you merge the first 2 arrays (size $2n$), then merge the 3rd array with that result (size $3n$), then the 4th (size $4n$), and so on up to the $k$-th array.</li><li><b>Exact Cost Derivation</b>:<ul><li>1st merge (size $n + n$): $2n$ operations</li><li>2nd merge (size $2n + n$): $3n$ operations</li><li>3rd merge (size $3n + n$): $4n$ operations</li><li>$(k-1)$-th merge (size $(k-1)n + n$): $kn$ operations</li><li>$\\text{Total Time} = 2n + 3n + 4n + \\dots + kn = n \\sum_{i=2}^{k} i = n\\left(\\frac{k(k+1)}{2} - 1\\right) = \\mathbf{\\Theta(n k^2)}$!</li></ul></li><li><b>How to Merge $k$ Sorted Arrays Faster in $\\Theta(nk \\lg k)$</b>: Pairwise merge the $k$ arrays in a balanced binary tournament tree (or use a Min-Heap of size $k$), performing $\\lg k$ rounds where each round merges all $kn$ elements in $\\Theta(nk)$ time $\\implies \\mathbf{\\Theta(nk \\lg k)}$!</li></ul>"
