          "notes": [
            {
              "h": "Simultaneous Min & Max in 1.5n − 2 Comparisons (Lab 3 PDF pp. 7–9 & Final 2019 Credit Q5)",
              "pts": [
                "<b>Why Naive Min + Max Takes $2n - 2$ Comparisons</b>: Finding `min` alone takes $n - 1$ comparisons, and finding `max` alone takes another $n - 1$ comparisons (total $2n - 2$). How can we reduce this to at most $\\mathbf{1.5n - 2}$ comparisons?",
                "<b>Algorithm 1 (Pairwise Comparison, Final 2019 Credit Q5 & Lab 3 p. 7)</b>:<ul><li><b>Step 1 ($n/2$ comparisons)</b>: Compare adjacent pairs `(A[2i], A[2i+1])` for $i = 0 \\dots n/2 - 1$. Put the smaller element of each pair in the ”min-candidates” group and the larger element in the ”max-candidates” group.</li><li><b>Step 2 ($n/2 - 1$ comparisons)</b>: Find the minimum among the $n/2$ min-candidates.</li><li><b>Step 3 ($n/2 - 1$ comparisons)</b>: Find the maximum among the $n/2$ max-candidates.</li><li><b>Total Comparisons</b>: $\\frac{n}{2} + \\left(\\frac{n}{2} - 1\\right) + \\left(\\frac{n}{2} - 1\\right) = \\mathbf{1.5n - 2}$ comparisons!</li></ul>",
                "<b>Algorithm 2 (Tournament Pair Merging on `[11, 3, 5, 7, 1, 9, 5, 2, 0, 8, 10, 13, 21, 5, 4, 1]`, Lab 3 pp. 7–9)</b>:<ul><li><b>Step 1 ($n/2 = 8$ comparisons)</b>: Sort each adjacent pair: `[3,11 | 5,7 | 1,9 | 2,5 | 0,8 | 10,13 | 5,21 | 1,4]`.</li><li><b>Step 2 (Merge pairs of pairs, comparing only firsts for min and lasts for max)</b>: Costs $2 + 4 + 8 + \\dots + n/2 = n - 2$ comparisons. Total $= n/2 + (n - 2) = \\mathbf{1.5n - 2}$ comparisons! Recurrence: $T(n) = 2T(n/2) + 2$ with $T(2) = 1 \\implies T(n) = 1.5n - 2$.</li></ul>"
              ]
            },
            {
              "h": "Four Signature Exam Divide-and-Conquer Workouts (Lab 3 PDF p. 9 & Midterms 2017–2025)",
              "pts": [
                "<b>Workout 1: Unimodal Array Maximum in $O(\\log n)$ Time (Lab 3 Ex. 1, Midterm 2017 Q7, Midterm 2018 Q7 & Exam Examples Q7)</b>:<ul><li>A unimodal array increases strictly up to its peak `A[p]` and decreases strictly thereafter (e.g., `[1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]`).</li><li><b>Algorithm</b>: At `mid = (low + high) / 2`:<ul><li>If `A[mid] > A[mid - 1]` and `A[mid] > A[mid + 1]`, return `A[mid]` (the peak!).</li><li>If `A[mid - 1] < A[mid] < A[mid + 1]` (on the rising slope), recurse <b>right</b> (`low = mid + 1`).</li><li>If `A[mid - 1] > A[mid] > A[mid + 1]` (on the falling slope), recurse <b>left</b> (`high = mid - 1`).</li></ul></li><li><b>Complexity</b>: $T(n) = T(n/2) + O(1) = \\mathbf{O(\\log n)}$.</li></ul>",
                "<b>Workout 2: Fixed-Point Search $A[i] = i$ in Sorted Distinct Integers in $O(\\log n)$ (Lab 3 Ex. 2 & Midterm 2022 Q3)</b>:<ul><li><b>Key Proof</b>: Because $A[1..n]$ consists of <b>distinct integers in strictly increasing order</b>, for every step to the right, $A[k]$ increases by <i>at least $1$</i> ($A[k+1] - A[k] \\ge 1$). Therefore, the difference $D(k) = A[k] - k$ is <b>monotonically non-decreasing</b>!<ul><li>If $A[mid] == mid$, return $mid$.</li><li>If $A[mid] > mid$, then for all $k > mid$, $A[k] \\ge A[mid] + (k - mid) > mid + (k - mid) = k$, so no fixed point can exist in the right half $\\implies$ recurse <b>left</b> (`high = mid - 1`).</li><li>If $A[mid] < mid$, then for all $k < mid$, $A[k] \\le A[mid] - (mid - k) < k$, so no fixed point can exist in the left half $\\implies$ recurse <b>right</b> (`low = mid + 1`).</li></ul></li><li><b>Complexity</b>: $T(n) = T(n/2) + O(1) = \\mathbf{O(\\log n)}$.</li></ul>",
                "<b>Workout 3: Second-Largest Element in $n + \\lg n - 2$ Comparisons (Lab 3 Ex. 3)</b>:<ul><li>Run a knockout tournament tree to find the maximum element in $n - 1$ comparisons (like a tennis tournament of $\\lg n$ rounds).</li><li>The <b>second-largest element</b> must have lost directly to the overall winner in one of the $\\lg n$ rounds! Finding the max among those $\\lg n$ direct losers takes $\\lg n - 1$ comparisons $\\implies$ Total $= (n - 1) + (\\lg n - 1) = \\mathbf{n + \\lg n - 2}$ comparisons!</li></ul>",
                "<b>Workout 4: Maximum Subarray Problem via Divide-and-Conquer (Midterm 2025 Q1)</b>:<ul><li>In Midterm 2025 Q1, `F2(A, l, mid, r)` scans left from `mid` down to `l` and right from `mid+1` up to `r` in $\\Theta(n)$ time to find the <b>maximum-sum subarray crossing the midpoint</b> (`left_sum + right_sum`).</li><li>`F1(A, l, r)` recursively computes `left_best = F1(A, l, mid)`, `right_best = F1(A, mid+1, r)`, and `cross_best = F2(A, l, mid, r)`, returning `max(left_best, right_best, cross_best)`.</li><li><b>Recurrence & Complexity</b>: $T(n) = 2T(n/2) + \\Theta(n) = \\mathbf{\\Theta(n \\lg n)}$!</li></ul>"
              ],
              "code": "// 1. Unimodal Array Maximum in O(log n) (Midterm 2017/2018 Q7)\nint findUnimodalMax(int A[], int low, int high) {\n    int mid = (low + high) / 2;\n    if (A[mid] > A[mid - 1] && A[mid] > A[mid + 1]) return A[mid];\n    if (A[mid] < A[mid + 1]) return findUnimodalMax(A, mid + 1, high); // rising\n    else return findUnimodalMax(A, low, mid - 1);                      // falling\n}\n\n// 2. Fixed-Point Search A[i] == i in O(log n) (Midterm 2022 Q3)\nint findFixedPoint(int A[], int low, int high) {\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = (low + high) / 2;\n        if (A[mid] == mid) return mid;\n        else if (A[mid] > mid) high = mid - 1;\n        else low = mid + 1;\n    }\n    return -1;\n}"
            }
          ],
