          "<b>Base n = 1</b>: $\\begin{pmatrix}F_2&F_1\\\\F_1&F_0\\end{pmatrix}$ = $\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.<br><b>Step n ≥ 2</b>: assume the statement for n − 1. Then $\\begin{pmatrix}F_n&F_{n-1}\\\\F_{n-1}&F_{n-2}\\end{pmatrix}$·$\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$ has entries F<sub>n</sub> + F<sub>n−1</sub> = F<sub>n+1</sub>, F<sub>n</sub>, F<sub>n−1</sub> + F<sub>n−2</sub> = F<sub>n</sub>, F<sub>n−1</sub>. So it equals $\\begin{pmatrix}F_{n+1}&F_n\\\\F_n&F_{n-1}\\end{pmatrix}$.<br>By the hypothesis the left matrix is M<sup>n−1</sup>, so the result is M<sup>n−1</sup>·M = M<sup>n</sup>. ∎",
