            "In practice radix sort is fast for large inputs, as well as simple to code and maintain.",
            "Example of slide 6.39, 32-bit numbers:<ul><li><b>at most 3 passes</b> when sorting ≥ 2000 numbers</li><li>for n = 2000, r = lg 2000 ≈ 10.97, so take r = 11 bits (2<sup>11</sup> = 2048), and ⌈32/11⌉ = 3 passes ‡</li><li>for larger n, r only grows, so the number of passes stays ≤ 3 ‡</li><li>merge sort and quicksort do at least ⌈lg 2000⌉ = <b>11 passes</b> over the data</li></ul>",
