            "Do not read \"3 passes versus 11\" as \"3.7 times faster\": each radix pass is a full counting sort, so it does more work per element than a pass of comparisons ‡.",
            "Downside: <b>locality of reference</b> means touching memory addresses that are near each other, which is what the small fast caches of a processor reward. Radix sort has little locality: every element jumps to some far position. ‡",
            "Modern processors have <b>steep memory hierarchies</b>: a tiny cache is much faster than main memory. So a well-tuned quicksort, which scans arrays from left to right and has good locality, fares better on them (slide 6.39). ‡"
