            "The <b>substitution method</b> is a proof by induction in steps:<ul><li>guess the form, for example T(n) ≤ cn</li><li>assume it for all smaller sizes (the <b>inductive hypothesis</b>)</li><li>substitute it into the recurrence</li><li>show the same form comes out, with the same c</li><li>check the base cases</li></ul>",
            "Tiny example: T(n) = T(n/2) + n, guess T(n) ≤ 2n. Assume T(n/2) ≤ 2(n/2) = n. Then T(n) ≤ n + n = 2n, the same form. It holds.",
            "<b>Desired minus residual</b>: write the right side as (what you want) − (something). With guess cn: T(n) ≤ c(n/2) + n = cn − (cn/2 − n). If the residual cn/2 − n is ≥ 0, we get T(n) ≤ cn. Here that needs c ≥ 2.",
            "Handling a Θ(n) term: it is at most dn for some constant d. So the residual just needs c to be large compared with d. Slides 7.16 and 7.29 use this.",
            "A trap: T(n) ≤ cn + n is not T(n) ≤ cn. The proof must end with the same c, or nothing is proved.",
